UP-8 — `gd.Polygon2d` ownership on `push_back`: documented, not "fixed"
==================================================================

SEVERITY: low.  FILE: 2 comments (+ 2 specs).  THIS IS A DOCUMENTATION PATCH.

WHY THIS ONE IS DIFFERENT FROM THE OTHERS
------------------------------------------
Every other finding in this package changes behaviour. This one does not, and
that is the deliberate result of an investigation that went the other way.

THE SITUATION
-------------
The collision-mask editor builds `gd::Polygon2d` objects in WASM and pushes
them into a `gd::VectorPolygon2d` that lives inside the frame's collision
masks:

```js
// newIDE/app/src/ObjectEditor/Editors/SpriteEditor/CollisionMasksEditor/PolygonsList.js
const polygon = new gd.Polygon2d();
...                                  // fill it
collisionMask.push_back(polygon);    // <-- is `polygon` ours to delete()?
```

The Emscripten convention says a constructed object must be `delete()`d or it
leaks. But `VectorPolygon2d::push_back` takes a copy into C++ memory — so the
JS wrapper becomes garbage, and deleting it looked like the obviously correct
thing to do.

IT IS NOT. Deleting it corrupts the WASM heap.

That is not a guess. We hit it, twice, and spent real time on it:

- deleting the pushed wrapper produced "memory access out of bounds" crashes
  in the sprite editor, reproducible from the same operation;
- the SAME delete is safe on the standalone `VectorPolygon2d` used by
  `PolygonHelper.spec`, where the vector is a local and not the live mask.

So the rule is real but **binding-specific**: whether the pushed wrapper may
be deleted depends on which vector received it, and nobody could tell that by
reading the call site. That asymmetry is exactly the kind of thing the next
maintainer gets wrong, and when they do it, the editor crashes rather than
leaking.

WHY WE DID NOT "FIX" IT
-----------------------
The obvious fix — add the missing `delete()` calls — is the one that crashes.
The alternative fix (copy semantics on the libGD side, so the ownership
question goes away) is a C++ change to `VectorPolygon2d`, which is a much
larger decision for the upstream project than this patch can make.

So the fix is to make the trap INHERENTLY VISIBLE:

1. **Document the ownership transfer at both editor sites**, in terms that
   say what happens if you get it wrong:

   ```js
   // Ownership transfer: `collisionMask` (the live mask vector) takes
   // ownership of the polygon pushed into it, and the JS wrapper must NOT be
   // deleted afterwards. Deleting a wrapper that was pushed into the live
   // mask vector CORRUPTS THE WASM HEAP — this was reproduced, not
   // theorised. See `createCollisionMaskFromBounds` below for the safe
   // pattern.
   ```

2. **Extract the add/mask logic into exported pure-ish helpers**
   (`addRectangleCollisionMaskTo`, `createCollisionMaskFromBounds`) so the
   ownership rule lives in one place instead of being repeated at each call
   site. Behaviour is identical; only the location of the code changed.

3. **Pin it with INVERSE regression tests** — this is the part worth
   stealing even if you apply nothing else:

   ```js
   expect(pushBackSpy).toHaveBeenCalled();
   expect(deleteSpy).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
   ```

   A test that asserts a leak does not happen cannot catch a deletion either.
   The test has to assert the ABSENCE of the leak. Anyone who later "fixes"
   the leak gets a red test that says why they should not.


WHAT WE ARE ASKING FOR
----------------------
Take the comments and the inverse tests. If upstream wants to make the
question disappear, that is a separate and welcome discussion about
`VectorPolygon2d`'s copy semantics on the libGD side — but it should be a
decision, not an accident discovered by whoever crashes the sprite editor
next.
